1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,130
A lot of things have been happening
on the internet this month.

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:07,150
October is supposed to be Cybersecurity
Awareness Month and it has ironically

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,440
been one attack after the other.

4
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:12,670
PHP, which is the language meant
most of the websites in the world,

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:16,000
has had two critical security holes.

6
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:19,730
Railway, which is also a popular platform
among developers where we deploy apps,

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:23,280
has had to literally tell their
users to change their secret keys.

8
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,560
There is also another platform, Zeabur,
that is literally shutting down after

9
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,650
being hacked twice, like
they're literally checking out.

10
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:35,000
And there is news of another hacker that
has used AI agents to break into more

11
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:39,700
than a hundred companies. So it's been
an overflow of attacks this month.

12
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:42,740
I believe the PHP one is
the biggest one so far.

13
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:46,540
PHP runs nearly three quarters
of the websites in the world.

14
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:49,780
WordPress runs on PHP.
Laravel runs on PHP.

15
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:54,140
I write Laravel at work.
Most of the web runs on PHP.

16
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:57,760
And two critical loopholes have
been found in PHP this month.

17
00:00:57,850 --> 00:00:59,690
They're calling it the PHP2Shell.

18
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:02,640
And the shell part of the name
is actually very important.

19
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:07,520
Getting a shell means that the attack is
so bad that an attacker can type commands

20
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:11,540
on your server as if they were
on their own personal computer.

21
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:13,390
PHP has rated this 9.2

22
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:16,500
out of 10, so it is really terrible.

23
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:17,940
Now there are two bugs reported.

24
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,700
The first one is about memory. So if PHP
runs out of memory at a very specific

25
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,420
moment while it's reading a request,
it leaves some unfinished data behind,

26
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:32,200
then it's cleanup code treats that broken
data like it's real and an attacker

27
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:34,240
can use it to run their own commands.

28
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:38,940
The second bug is more about leftover
information like leftover stuff on PHP

29
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:42,760
workers from previous requests
that it should have thrown away.

30
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:44,460
Now these are not just random bugs.

31
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:48,450
These are huge loopholes that people
have already tried to exploit.

32
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:53,360
The thing about PHP2Shell. and why it's
really so crazy is that it happens before

33
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:58,957
your code even runs. So PHP reads
the incoming request first and this

34
00:01:58,977 --> 00:02:01,100
loophole is in that reading part.

35
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,180
So your login, your middleware,
your authentication,

36
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:06,380
nobody stands a chance against this stuff.

37
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:09,690
You can have the best login in
the world and it wouldn't matter.

38
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:13,180
Even Amazon has reported that someone
tried to use it on one of their honeypot

39
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,040
servers, which is basically a trap server
set up to catch attackers just four

40
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:18,660
days before it was even announced.

41
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:22,670
And there are government websites in
Europe that literally went off while they

42
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,670
patched because of all of this drama.

43
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:28,440
So basically if you're running PHP
anywhere, you have to update your PHP.

44
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:33,480
If you're still on PHP 8.1 or older, you
need to upgrade and updating the package

45
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:38,000
is not even enough. You have to restart
your Apache and if you use Docker,

46
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:39,580
you have to rebuild your images.

47
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,550
Everyone has been patching and if
you're using Laravel for example,

48
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:46,180
your Laravel version doesn't even protect
you here because the loophole is in

49
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:47,890
PHP which is underneath Laravel.

50
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:52,240
And it's not even just PHP, Node.js also
had its own security release on that

51
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:55,060
same day. So if you run Node,
you need to update that too.

52
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:56,890
Now why everyone was busy patching

53
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,560
PHP? Railway happened on the same day.

54
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:02,700
If you don't know what Railway is,
Railway is a deployment platform where you

55
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:03,870
can deploy your apps.

56
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:08,220
So basically, Railway says someone may
have accessed their customers' encrypted

57
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:11,900
environment variables and if you're a
developer, you know how important it is

58
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,710
to keep your environment variable safe.

59
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,270
So Railway paused deployments for
a while, this even affected me,

60
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:21,460
and they asked their users to revoke and
change their API keys and cloud keys.

61
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:25,000
There's still no full details from
Railway on how the attackers got in.

62
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,320
They did say however, that it's part

63
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,320
of a series of attacks

64
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:33,210
on platforms like theirs with the
goal of getting to AI companies.

65
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:38,080
Now Zeabur, the company that is shutting down
because of attacks is a very interesting

66
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:43,250
one. This is also another platform that
developers use to deploy apps and they

67
00:03:43,270 --> 00:03:45,000
are deciding to shut down completely.

68
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:48,220
They got breached in August
and on October 14th and 15th,

69
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:51,680
they got breached again, which
leaked internal credentials.

70
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:55,080
The second breach covered about 321,810

71
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:00,450
accounts and about 3.2 million
environment variables records.

72
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:03,940
So how did the attackers get in the
second time after they got in the first

73
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,700
time? According to Zeabur, their
admin token had no expiry date.

74
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,820
So they had not changed this
token since January 2024.

75
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:14,120
So after the first attack,
when they changed their keys,

76
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,540
they didn't include that
particular set of tokens.

77
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:17,910
They didn't change those set

78
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:23,140
and that kind of opened the gates again
and the old platform now is deciding

79
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:26,200
to shut down. They will pause services
and just shut everything down by the

80
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:28,100
end of December. This is crazy.

81
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:33,400
The OpenCode one is also very, very
interesting because people found out OpenCode

82
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,220
had been hacked from their bills.

83
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:38,030
OpenCode is an open source AI coding tool.

84
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:42,760
It has an online platform called OpenCode
Console where people store their AI

85
00:04:42,780 --> 00:04:47,320
API keys. Now OpenCode said attackers
broke into an older system and took those

86
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,093
keys. So people started
getting alerts from OpenAI,

87
00:04:50,113 --> 00:04:53,660
Anthropic, Google, and some of these
charges were in hundreds of dollars.

88
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:58,420
A few of them were even over a thousand
dollars for AI usage that users did

89
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:01,870
not run. Like imagine getting
alerts for things you didn't use.

90
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:03,250
OpenCode calls it a distillation

91
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:08,200
attack. That is when someone asks an AI
model loads of questions and just uses

92
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,800
the answer to train their own model.

93
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:15,120
So basically in simple words someone
may have been building their own AI on

94
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,440
other people's bill. Of course the AI
companies have blocked the stolen keys

95
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,660
and refunds are going out, which is good.

96
00:05:20,850 --> 00:05:24,540
When you put the Railway, Zeabur
and OpenCode incidents side by side,

97
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:26,440
you realize they are kind
of the same pattern.

98
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:30,600
In these situations particularly the
attackers went straight for the place where

99
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:32,500
developers keep their secret keys.

100
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:35,320
So if you use any of this platform you
want to change any key you're not sure

101
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:39,700
about. You want to give your keys an expiry
date whenever or wherever the service

102
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:43,380
allows it. You want to give each
keys only the access it needs.

103
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:47,380
So if a key only needs to read one
storage bucket just give it access to that

104
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:53,160
one storage bucket. Set spending limits,
set alerts on all your AI and cloud

105
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:57,250
accounts and honestly do not store
keys in tools that don't need them.

106
00:05:57,370 --> 00:06:02,340
Now there's this one more story from
the October drama and this one actually

107
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:07,260
sounds so made up. eMudhra is one of the
companies their browsers trust to confirm

108
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,180
that a website is who
it really says it is.

109
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:12,150
That is how important this company is.

110
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:15,440
So it's like this little padlock
that your browser depends on.

111
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:20,630
Now it turns out that eight of
their signing keys were so weak.

112
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:25,060
The keys were so weak that one of
them literally was the number four.

113
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:26,530
Just number four.

114
00:06:27,030 --> 00:06:28,700
That's how weak their keys were.

115
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:29,720
Like just

116
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,800
number four. So the scripts went wrong
when the keys were made in April 2025

117
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:37,620
and it made a second set of weak keys.

118
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:41,560
And with keys like this of course someone
could fake a website and your browser

119
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:42,810
will still have shown

120
00:06:43,470 --> 00:06:46,700
the padlock. To be honest in cases like
this I don't think there is anything

121
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:50,820
the average users can even do about
these things but just to keep your stuff

122
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:53,280
updated. And I think in
the middle of all of this

123
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:59,290
attack drama is where the role
that AI plays on all sides.

124
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:04,920
Reuters reported that one attacker used
AI agents to break into more than a

125
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:08,850
hundred company websites. These
include sites belonging to SharkNinja,

126
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:12,820
Cummins, all of those. So in this case
what the attackers did was that it gave

127
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:16,550
the agent short instructions and just
let them run on for hours on their own.

128
00:07:16,570 --> 00:07:21,030
And the cost estimated to about $50
in AI tokens per successful hack.

129
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:25,420
Like it's so crazy that we've gotten
to the point where it's costing $50 to

130
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:29,870
hack into a company. It's just been so
interesting reading and seeing the news

131
00:07:30,070 --> 00:07:34,900
everywhere. What I find even interesting
is how AI is literally on both sides.

132
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:39,530
For example the PHP2Shell loopholes
were found with help from AI.

133
00:07:39,730 --> 00:07:41,780
And AI is also being
used by the attackers.

134
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:45,720
So the same tools that are helping the
defenders find the bugs faster are also

135
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:48,380
helping the attackers break in faster.

136
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,740
So it's almost like this
back and forth situation.

137
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:54,392
I have people that are working in companies
that have literally been stuck over

138
00:07:54,412 --> 00:07:57,670
dealing with attacks, it's
been crazy, really crazy.
